{
  "dom_id": "step-q25305",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find the roots of $f(h)=\\frac{3{h}^{2}+18}{{h}^{2}+10h+25}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(h)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(h)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{h}^{2}+18}{{h}^{2}+10h+25}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor $f(h),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & =\\frac{3{h}^{2}+18}{{h}^{2}+10h+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({h}^{2}+6)}{(h+5)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $h:$\n\n$$\n3({h}^{2}+6)=0\n$$\n\nSince ${h}^{2}+6>0$ for all $h,$ there is no real solution to $f(h)=0.$ Therefore, $f(h)$ has no real roots.",
